/*****************************************************************************
    jitXe2 -- E2 CHARACTERISED: A MID-BODY RETURN INSIDE AN INLINED CALLEE.
    2026-08-08. SEQ 41 step 5, item 1.

    ⚠ THE CASE NOTHING IN THE FLEET HAS EVER RUN. Three things look like this
    one and none of them is it:
      · rung JRt4  -- mid-block return, but in the DRIVER action, not inlined.
                      Green, degrade 0. Different construct.
      · rung JXT   -- return inside an inlined callee, but in TAIL position.
                      Green with degrade 2, and green BY ACCIDENT: E2's unbuilt
                      thing is the BRANCH to the enclosing epilogue, and a tail
                      return needs no branch, so falling through is equivalent.
      · jitXseq    -- two inlined callees, both tail returns. Same accident.
    So the fleet's evidence about E2 is entirely from the ONE position where
    E2 does not matter. That is what this fixture fixes.

    THE CONSTRUCT: a callee that returns EARLY, with live statements after the
    return that must NOT run.

        xeCallee:  if xeIn > 0;  return 111;
                   xeTail = 999;
                   return 222;

    THE PREDICTION, and it is causal so it is worth writing down before the
    run (this project's causal claims are roughly a coin flip): the emit walk
    degrades the return statement rather than emitting a branch, so the early
    exit is not taken, the tail runs anyway, and fire 1 answers 222 with
    xeTail = 999. WRONG, and SILENT -- degrade fires but degrading a mid-body
    return is not equivalent the way degrading a tail return is.

    xeTail IS THE SECOND CHANNEL AND IT IS LOAD-BEARING (one channel, one
    meaning). A returned value alone cannot distinguish "the early return was
    taken" from "the tail ran and its value was discarded" -- both could
    plausibly yield 111. xeTail answers the question the value cannot.

    THE TWO FIRES TAKE OPPOSITE ARMS, so neither a folded constant nor a
    default satisfies both:
        fire 1  xeIn =  1  ->  111, xeTail 0     (early return TAKEN)
        fire 2  xeIn = -1  ->  222, xeTail 999   (early return NOT taken)

    DIAGNOSTIC:
        111/0 then 222/999   E2 WORKS -- the branch is emitted
        222/999 both fires   E2 UNBUILT -- early return ignored, tail always runs
        111/999              the branch was taken AND the tail ran: worse than
                             either, and would mean the emitted control flow
                             falls through into dead code

    ORDER (bear-trap #25): jitted half FIRST -- testing() routes on isCoded and
    an interpreted run consumes it. Oracle last, and read as a FIELD plus a
    returned value; bear-trap #25's sibling says a post-jit interpreted call is
    not a clean oracle for a RETURNED value, so xeTail (a field) is the trusted
    half of the oracle and the returned value is recorded, not leaned on.

    SENTINEL AT THE FOOT (H2). Run under a wall-clock cap (H5).
*****************************************************************************/
Start();
include(unitTests);
include(generate);
include(utilities);

search reset stack Grokking;
search Generating UnitTests bcOPs Utilities list;

register(JitXe2);
define
    xeIn;
    xeOut;
    xeTail;
    xeCallee code={
        if xeIn > 0;
            return 111;
        xeTail = 999;
        return 222;
        };
    xeDriver code={
        xeOut = xeCallee();
        };
    ;

xeIn   = 1;
xeOut  = 0;
xeTail = 0;
print "XE fire 1 setup : xeIn =" xeIn " (early return SHOULD be taken)":;
testing(xeDriver);
print "XE fire 1 result: xeOut =" xeOut " (want 111) xeTail =" xeTail " (want 0)":;

xeIn   = -1;
xeOut  = 0;
xeTail = 0;
print "XE fire 2 setup : xeIn =" xeIn " (early return should NOT be taken, recompile: NONE)":;
jitRefire();
print "XE fire 2 result: xeOut =" xeOut " (want 222) xeTail =" xeTail " (want 999)":;

xeIn   = 1;
xeOut  = 0;
xeTail = 0;
xeDriver();
print "XE interpreted  : xeOut =" xeOut " (oracle) xeTail =" xeTail " (oracle -- want 0)":;

print "XE SENTINEL -- run reached the end":;
stop();
